1
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:11,000
From market records to a forecast of realized variance.
A conceptual view of the RP4 v4 method.

2
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:23,000
FMP supplies one-minute stock bars. Unusual Whales supplies options records.
Fields below are examples of inputs; no observed records are displayed.

3
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:37,000
Quality and source-time rules determine which records are eligible.
The 120-second cutoff is an availability proxy, not verified client receipt.

4
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:49,000
Past closes become log returns, then squared returns and backward-looking sums.
Volume, market controls and session timing complete B0.

5
00:00:49,000 --> 00:01:01,000
Options are grouped by maturity and moneyness.
Weighted IV summaries and smile measures extend B0 into B1.

6
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:15,000
B2 adds activity, option-state changes, exposure proxies and empty-window handling.
A valid empty activity window is different from unavailable provider data.

7
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:27,000
Each row aligns an asset, a session and a forecast minute.
The comparisons use nested inputs at matching forecast origins.

8
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:41,000
Log ridge combines transformed predictors with regularized weights.
LightGBM builds additive trees. The two families produce separate forecasts.

9
00:01:41,000 --> 00:01:53,000
Training expands through time; recent training sessions select settings.
Training-only transformations and a 60-minute purge/embargo protect evaluation.

10
00:01:53,000 --> 00:02:05,000
The forecast is fixed before future returns are revealed.
Realized variance is then the sum of squared one-minute log returns.


